    ## High-level layout under src/ai

    Given your constraints (no extra deep nesting, `src/ai` is the Heart), a practical, “next‑gen but sane” layout is:

    - `src/ai/orchestration/` – the mainOrchestrator and orchestration policies (the “brain” of ZacAi).  
    - `src/ai/core/` – core system model, self-healing engine, bootstrap flows, and shared contracts that make the Heart self-reliant.  
    - `src/ai/input-processing/` – adapters, parsing, normalization, classification.  
    - `src/ai/embeddings/` – embedding pipelines, vector backends, and related configs.  
    - `src/ai/inference-engine/` – model runners, routing to different LMs/multimodal models.  
    - `src/ai/monitoring/` – metrics, logs, telemetry, anomaly detectors.  
    - `src/ai/tools/` – calculators, URL fetchers, CLI executor, file access, etc.  

    This keeps depth at two or three levels while giving you explicit separation of concerns: orchestration logic and system self-awareness live in `orchestration` and `core`, while the rest are pluggable subsystems.[1][4] Industry guidance for scalable projects is exactly this: a shallow `src/` with a `core/` or equivalent that holds the stable business logic, and feature/infra modules around it.[1][2]

    ## What goes in src/ai/core vs src/ai/orchestration

    To make the Heart “concrete bulletproof,” define clear responsibilities:

    **`src/ai/core/` (immutable backbone)**  
    - `system-model/`  
      - Static and dynamic representation of the ZacAi stack: components, dependencies, capabilities, and health states.  
        - Typed interfaces for “what ZacAi is” so orchestration code never hardcodes paths or service names.  
        - `boot/`  
          - Bootstrap graph: what must come online in what order; minimal “safe mode” where only the Heart and a tiny config store exist.  
            - Offline initialization flows: how the Heart behaves if embeddings, monitoring, or external agents don’t respond.  
            - `self-heal/`  
              - Detection–diagnosis–correction loop primitives (interfaces for detectors, RCAs, and remediation actions).[5][6]  
                - Remediation playbooks encoded as data + small handlers (restart module, reconfigure, fall back, degrade mode).  
                - `contracts/`  
                  - Shared types and schemas for tasks, plans, tool calls, health events, and logs.  
                    - This ensures all other folders speak the Heart’s language without the Heart depending on their internal layout.  

                    **`src/ai/orchestration/` (the living Heart)**  
                    - `goal-interpreter.ts` – converts any incoming prompt or system event into a `Goal { type, constraints, successCriteria }`.  
                    - `planner/` – builds multi-step plans and subplans over the system model; chooses which modules/tools to use and when.[4][7]  
                    - `policy-engine/` – routing and safety rules; when to call a tool vs rely on internal reasoning, when to enter “safe mode.”  
                    - `executor/` – runs plans step-by-step, calls into `inference-engine`, `tools`, `monitoring`, handles retries and fallbacks.  
                    - `diag-controller/` – owns continuous health and self-healing loops, consuming signals from `monitoring` and invoking `core/self-heal`.  

                    This way, `orchestration` is the active manager, but all its world-model and invariants live in `core`, making it easier to reason about and test.[3][4]

